diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — web2.local and web3.local failing to load from host_1_1_1_1 — was investigated exhaustively across all network layers and **no anomaly was found**. The diagnostic sweep covered: (A) L2 identity and infrastructure — infra_sweep reported zero ACL rules, zero static ARP entries, no addressing or resolver divergence across all 15 devices; l2_snapshot confirmed no duplicate MACs and all interfaces clean. (B) Control-plane and routing — all six FRR routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, switch_server_access) are fully healthy with active vtysh sockets and all OSPF neighbors in Full state; tc_snapshot confirmed all 30 interfaces run only the default fq_codel qdisc with no shaping or delay applied. (D) Service and resolution — service_snapshot directly tested the claimed failure scenario: from host_1_1_1_1, DNS resolves web2.local → 10.200.0.5 and web3.local → 10.200.0.6 (both correct), and HTTP GET to both URLs returns 200 OK in 3–4 ms; all four web servers answer 200 on localhost; pressure_sweep shows no stress tool, CPU spike, or socket exhaustion on any device. The user's report is unconfirmed by any observable evidence. There is no fault mechanism present in the current network state, and no historical trace (flap scripts, carrier-change counters, syslog entries) of a past transient. The most likely explanation is that the issue was transient and has self-resolved, or the test was performed from a different host or browser context at the time of the ticket.
